      "name": "itinerary.md",
      "mimeType": "text/markdown",
      "text": "# Hangzhou weekend: slow walks and Longjing tea\n\nTwo people, Saturday morning to Sunday night. The time blocks are a flexible template for any weekend; choose trains after the weekend is set. The plan assumes Shanghai Hongqiao–Hangzhou East high-speed rail, one shared double/twin room near Hubin, and no paid attractions.\n\n## Saturday — West Lake at walking pace\n\n- **Morning:** Take a morning train from Shanghai Hongqiao to Hangzhou East (roughly an hour; check the actual timetable when booking). Drop bags at a hotel around Fengqi Road or Longxiangqiao, then walk towards the lake.\n- **Late morning–lunch:** Start at Hubin and follow the north shore via Broken Bridge, Bai Causeway and Gushan. Stop for a simple noodle lunch around Gushan or Hubin; leave time to sit down.\n- **Afternoon:** Continue at an easy pace along Su Causeway to Huagang Guanyu. This makes a full lakeside day of about 8–10 km on foot, with plenty of places to pause. Shorten the route if you would rather spend longer by the water.\n- **Evening:** Have dinner around Qinghefang/Hefang Street and take an unhurried look along the old street before returning to the hotel.\n\n## Sunday — Jiuxi to Longjing, then tea\n\n- **Morning:** Check out and leave bags with the hotel. Take a taxi or bus to the Jiuxi trail entrance. Walk from Jiuxi Yanshu through Jiuxi Eighteen Streams to Longjing Village; the published route is about 3.8 km. It is a good relaxed walk with breaks, not the longer, steeper Shili Langdang hike.\n- **Lunch:** Eat in or near the village; share a couple of local dishes and keep the meal to about ¥80–100 per person.\n- **Early afternoon:** Sit for tea in Longjing Village for 1–2 hours. Longjing Caiguan (龍井菜馆, Longjing Village No. 1) is one listed option with terrace seating for tea and food. Check the menu and the per-person or minimum spend before ordering; the listing’s average spend is for a meal, not a tea-only price. A ¥150-per-person tea allowance is set aside in the budget.\n- **Later afternoon:** Take a short village-lane walk if you feel like it, then return to the hotel for bags and continue to Hangzhou East. Leave a generous transfer margin and choose a return train that gets you to Shanghai before late night. Verify the day’s bus service and train times before you go.\n\n杭州公交 27/27B and 87 serve the Longjing area; the exact stop and service can vary, so check the live route on the day. A taxi for the Jiuxi start or the station transfer makes the plan simpler if you want to protect the tea time.\n\n## Cost assumptions\n\nThe paired budget is in `budget.csv`. It targets ¥1,280 per person and caps planned spending at ¥1,420 per person, leaving ¥80 below the ¥1,500 limit. The lodging cap is ¥700 for one room for two. Since no weekend is selected, these are booking limits, not live hotel or train quotes. If a room exceeds its cap, choose a different area/property before booking.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [12306 Shanghai–Hangzhou fare notice](https://www.12306.cn/mormhweb/zxdt/202405/t20240502_41893.html): Shanghai Hongqiao–Hangzhou East second class published fare is ¥87 each way; discounts vary by train and the actual fare is shown at purchase.\n- [Hangzhou bus service update](https://www.hzbus.cn/hzbus/detail/36/7618) and [Hangzhou West Lake bus routes](https://wgly.hangzhou.gov.cn/art/2022/12/6/art_1229695828_58943250.html): route information for buses serving Longjing and the lake area.\n- [Jiuxi–Longjing walking route](https://www.hzzx.gov.cn/cshz/content/2024-07/08/content_8755727.htm): the route through Jiuxi Eighteen Streams to Longjing Village is about 3.8 km.\n- [Longjing Caiguan listing](https://you.ctrip.com/food/14/240882.html): location and terrace seating; its reported average spend is not a guaranteed tea price.\n",
